{
  "image_name": "2b76cec8e248b63052b8265ce26e97e7.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a target cell is either protected from or marked for phagocytic clearance through opposing surface signals. On the left, “don’t-eat-me” cues on the target cell—such as CD47, MHC class I, CD24, and sialylated proteins or lipids—engage inhibitory receptors on the phagocyte, including SIRPα, LILRB1, SIGLEC10, and related SIGLECs. These interactions are shown in red and represent mechanisms that suppress engulfment.\n\nOn the right, green “eat-me” signals promote recognition and uptake. These include desialylated proteins or lipids and phosphatidylserine, which are detected either directly or through bridging molecules. The diagram indicates that removal of sialic acid by desialylation converts a previously inhibitory, sialylated surface into a pro-phagocytic one. Desialylated structures appear to be recognized by galactose or mannose receptors and by opsonin-like factors such as GAL3, calreticulin, and complement components, which then engage receptors like MERTK, LRP1, CR1/CR3, TREM2, GPR56, and TIMs. Phosphatidylserine is also linked to bridging molecules such as GAS6, C1q, and MFGE8.\n\nOverall, the image presents phagocytosis as a balance between inhibitory and activating recognition pathways at the cell surface.",
